    p.author #[span.me Angelo Nardone]
    h4.title.mb-1
      | Lossless Text Compression Using LLMs
    .btn-group.btn-group-sm(role="group",aria-label="Commands").mt-1
      
      button.btn.btn-primary(type="button",data-bs-toggle="collapse",data-bs-target="#talk-5bd4448e75cf7acf96a93a6242a42983",aria-expanded="false",aria-controls="talk-5bd4448e75cf7acf96a93a6242a42983")
        | #[i.bi.bi-file-earmark-text-fill] Abstract
      a(href="slides/Lossless Text Compression Using LLMs.pdf",target="_blank").btn.btn-primary
        | #[i.bi.bi-easel3-fill] Slides
  .col-md-7
    p.abstract#talk-5bd4448e75cf7acf96a93a6242a42983.collapse.mt-2
        | Lossless text compression is a well-established field, where decades of research have led to highly optimized algorithms that approach practical limits in compression ratio. However, these standard methods often rely on local statistical patterns and struggle to capture deeper linguistic and grammatical structures present in natural language. In this talk, we explore a new direction based on Large Language Models (LLMs), which leverage their ability to model long-range dependencies and semantic regularities in text. Building on the Symbol Ranking Compression framework introduced by Shannon in 1951, we show that LLMs, thanks to their strong predictive capabilities, are a natural fit for this setting. Using recent methods as a reference point, we show that these techniques can achieve improved compression ratios compared to traditional compressors. At the same time, this gain comes with a significant cost in throughput, highlighting a key trade-off between compression efficiency and computational feasibility. The talk concludes by discussing the central challenge for future research: how to scale these methods to practical settings while preserving their compression advantages.
