    p.author #[span.me Andrea Castagna]
    h4.title.mb-1
      | Reordering text collections for better compression
    .btn-group.btn-group-sm(role="group",aria-label="Commands").mt-1
      
      button.btn.btn-primary(type="button",data-bs-toggle="collapse",data-bs-target="#talk-ea067ded72dd25acd6701036ff0c5996",aria-expanded="false",aria-controls="talk-ea067ded72dd25acd6701036ff0c5996")
        | #[i.bi.bi-file-earmark-text-fill] Abstract
      a(href="slides/Reordering text collections for better compression.pdf",target="_blank").btn.btn-primary
        | #[i.bi.bi-easel3-fill] Slides
  .col-md-7
    p.abstract#talk-ea067ded72dd25acd6701036ff0c5996.collapse.mt-2
        | Text compression algorithms in theory assume the text as a whole, but real implementations operate on separate blocks or shifting windows, considering only a portion of the preceding text. This design relies on the locality of patterns within blocks or windows but can hinder overall compressibility, especially when similar sections are far apart. The issue is even more apparent when compressing multiple texts together, as input order is inherently arbitrary. In this talk, we will describe some traditional compression techniques, look at how practical implementation differ from their theoretical definition. Then we will examine the problem of reordering texts to improve compressibility, looking at existing heuristic approaches, try to define a formal description of the problem and show that under standard assumptions finding an optimal solution is computationally intractable. Lastly we will discuss how this conclusion further motivates the search for heuristics and approximation algorithms.